A||||26||Mach10||Forensics?!? Am I totally insane?||||5||
Z||000000||Mach10||07-15-2001||08:31 PM||||There's a few things I'd like to sort of discuss, on an idle sunday. <BR>I'm sure I haven't mentioned this, but I am heading into my 3rd year of Anthropology at university. <BR>I started my educational career with an interest in mechanical engineering. This did not work. I don't have any natural affinity with numbers, nor the discipline required to fully learn them. Calculus may as well be writen in Greek. (Er, more so...) So, I switched focus to my other interest: Computers. I did the compSci bit for a while, but it became clear that my math skills (lack thereof) were hurting me. Top this off with an inability to concentrate for any length of time necessary for proper study. So, at the age of 18, I was diagnosed with ADD and hit with a Ritalin prescription. I lived on that for a while, and things improved a little, but the side-effects were a little bothersome, so I trained myself off the pills. I still have 'em if I need then. This is 3 years into school, and still taking mostly 1st and 2nd year courses, when I took up an Anthro course as a "General Interest" option.<BR>One term later, I was hooked. Anthropology is by far the most fun I have ever had in school. I dropped all my science courses, and transfered over to the Faculty of Arts: Majoring in Anthropology. I took my first year requirements in the first term, and started my second year in the second term, carrying over some of my science credits. Now I am taking the remainder of my 2nd year courses, and starting some 3rd year.<BR>I did some research as to career opportunities in this feild. Aside from the obvious "Indiana Jones" and "Tombraider" types, there wasn't too much. My speciality is physical anthropology, and I found that there is quite a lucrative career in Forensic Anthropology. I am enjoying the schooling very much, but I am a bit apprehensive about the job itself. Dealing exclusively with unidentified skeletons is not a normal occupation. The worst part, for me, is that I can't wait to get in the feild. Is this necesarily wrong? I justify this to myself by figuring that I am helping people. I guess my clients will be a little beyond help, but I don't figure that I'd ever rest easy without a proper burial, or with my killer still on the loose. I also think of the families, years of searching for a lost loved one. Although painful, I figure that closure would be the only way to truly heal.<P>Sorry for the book, people, just venting off a little post-teen angst. Feel free to comment, one way or another.||||reg||5
Z||000001||SteveJ||07-15-2001||09:16 PM||||Sounds like fun, go for it. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||07-15-2001||09:43 PM||||Not a thing wrong with that.<BR>Forensics is a fascinating science. <BR>At one time, my wife was considering sending an application to the GBI to work in the Crime Lab. (Chemistry and physics background.)<BR>Didn't, for monetary reasons and the commute distance.<BR>Aside from that, you would be providing a valuable service. And not just for victims' families. <BR>You might actually find the "piece of the puzzle" that could prevent someone else from falling victim. THAT would be a valuable service.<BR>Good luck! Go for it! <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||14
Z||000003||Pontiaddict||07-15-2001||09:52 PM||||It sounds like you found your calling.<BR>Good Luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||07-16-2001||01:12 PM||||Hey thats sounds great! I have always said the if you don't like your job your not going to be happy for 2/3rds of your life, screw that! <BR>I have tried about 30 different fields before I ended up doing computer support. I was an auto mechanic for a few years but it was to dirty so I tried my hand at Helecopter / Aircraft mechanic and found it to be not as dirty but the idea of working in an unairconditioned environment did not appeal to me. after several other carear changes I finaly ended up in the computer field where I have been for about 10 years now. I Enjoy what I do  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000005||JSocha||07-16-2001||03:06 PM||||We could always nickname you "QUINCY" thereafter and as you progress.<P>Herewith, be forwarned my friend not to bring your job home with you.  Especially if you get (are) married someday (already).<P>Don't think your wife would appreciate finding skeletons in your closet!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||07-16-2001||04:23 PM||||Hey that would be cool - when someone asks you what you do for a living, open your eyes real wide and whisper "I see dead people"<P>  im not sure where the line is between this and a coroner or medical examiner - when I was in the Coast Guard our most unpleasant duty was picking up corpses that were in the water.<P>  usually if someone drounds they sink to the bottom - several days later the corpse starts to bloat and floats back up - unless the water is really cold - then it might take months to come up. They were always extreemly gross.<P>  needless to say none of us liked getting those calls - so my only advice would be make sure you understand the whole scope of what you are getting into - skeletons? fire victims? dead bodies that still have decaying flesh on them? ||||reg||10
Z||000007||Shiner||07-16-2001||05:05 PM||||If it intrests you and makes you happy, go for it!  There is nothing wierd about it, unless you have some kind of cadaver fetish  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||frontal lobe||07-16-2001||06:43 PM||||No, not TOTALLY insane.  Just partly (j/k).  No, nothing wrong with being fascinated by it, as long as you can keep somewhere in the back of your mind that at one time this was part of a living person who had a real life just like you do (you do have a life, right?  another j/k).  And I don't think you will have a problem doing that.  So just go for it, and enjoy your new job!||||reg||1
Z||000009||hugh||07-16-2001||08:15 PM||||I look forward to seeing you on Forensic Detectives someday.On a more serious note;There is nothing more satisfying than doing a job that is challenging and gives a sense of accomplishment.Not everyone is lucky enough to have that kind of job.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DaRkLoRD||07-16-2001||08:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Shiner:<BR><B>There is nothing wierd about it, unless you have some kind of cadaver fetish   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think a lot of people have that.... mmm... flushmount headlights.... LOL  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||10
Z||000011||Mach10||07-17-2001||02:04 AM||||Ken: I assure that I was MUCH happier not knowing what "Degloving" is.<P>A Forensic Anthropolgist will deal mostly with completely or partially skeletonized remains. This includes complete or incomplete remains. My job would be to assemble as much as possible the remains, then gather a physical profile of the person, inlcuding sex, approximate age at death, estimated time of death, and cause of death. I'd be the one sorting out puncture wounds to bone tissue as before or after death (scavenger activity). Biggest jobs would be aircraft accidents and mass graves. Someone has to sort out each bone as beloning to which individual.<P>I haven't studied it at school, but as an extracurricular activity, I looked into forensic Entomology, and some studies on the process of decomposition. The worst part, for me is not the rotting bit. That's how you get yoghurt. Dead animals don't bother me, except for the smell. <BR>It's the knowledge that the corpse on the examining table was once a person, with dreams, feelings, and a sense of being. Not quite a spiritual thing, but I consider myself quite a humanist. <BR>That's why I don't like hitting people. I know exactly how much the strike will hurt.<P>Edit:<BR>BTW Ken: I always understood that in salt water, the corpse would float and dehydrate due to the salt content, THEN sink. After some time, the body would puterify (turn to liquid) THEN bloat and float. In fresh water, the body sinks, then starts swelling due to osmotic pressure as a result of the salt content of the bodily fluids. <BR>Either way, when they get back to the surface, it ain't pretty.<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 07-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||07-17-2001||11:18 AM||||maybe some of the people on here wont understand this - we were a bunch of 18 to 22 year old kids out on rescue boat crew - and we all signed up to rescue people - not to pick up dead bodies - but it was part of our jobs<P>  we de-personalized it completely - we did NOT think of them as being people - we called them 'Floaters' and we joked about it as much as possible amoungst ourselves when we had to deal with it, and we never talked about it when we didnt have to.<P>  One of our rescue crewmen caught bloody H#ll from the station captain one day when he said, "We have the floater secured onboard" on the VHF marine radio (on the coast guard only channel - I was on the other end of the radio, back at the station at the time).<P>  the captain chewed him out good when he got back - not for calling the victim a floater, and not for making light of the situation - but for using the word on the marine radio - which anyone can pickup with a scanner - we had to call em 'victim' in public<P>  but the captain also understood we had to keep our sanity - and we had to sheild ourselves from the fact that the stinking blob of goo and bones where were carrying on our squeaky clean deck was once a living person - just like us.<P>  and you never forget that smell - I was only in for 4 years and got out 21 years ago - but even today when I drive around in the summer i can tell in an instant when there is a dead animal somewhere along the side of the road - I dont have to see it - you never forget that smell.<P>  I realize I am putting up the downside of what you are looking at for a career - Im not trying to discourage you - but I would give the opposite advice to what others are saying here - the families of dead people expect dignity and respect for the remains of the dead - but the people that have to handle and deal with corpses need to disconnect themselves from the horror of what is in front of them - otherwise it would drive you insane.<P>  You can go outside and pick up a handfull of dirt, and some of what is in your hand use to be part of a living person or animal at some point the the past, maybe just a few molecules. In the Coast Guard we believed a person is their soul or their spirit, and what we were handling was 'ashes to ashes and dust to dust*' - not a 'person'.<P>  * or in our case - dust to mud %c)||||reg||1
Z||000013||Old Lar||07-17-2001||12:20 PM||||Anthropology as a career although interesting is one that, as you have discovered, has limited applications. The forensic aspects are a good opportunity to earn a real good living.  The gore part is something you will adjust to.  I was a medic in Viet-Nam, assigned to a pathologist, many years ago. As Ken stated there are certain oders that never leave you and death is one of them.  And in the jungle, bodies got "ripe" really quickly. It is something I did as it was expected.  I don't know how, now that I think back to those days.  I still remember the name, rank and condition of the first one that came in.<P>One of the pathologists wanted to go into forensic pathology. Lots of opportunity.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Mach10||07-17-2001||12:53 PM||||Ken: I am a creature of balance. Pointing out "the bad sides" actually strengthens my resolve. I need balance in my life, and having a job that is "too perfect" will not keep me entertained (so to speak) for very long. While it's OK to enjoy work, there has to be some element of personal challenge. I appreciate the input.<P>I understand the need for depersonification (did I just invent a word?) in cases like that. Fortunately, my line of work will mostly involve people that have been in the ground a long time. The other types will have been burned, which is possibly even more horrible, but less smelly. More importantly, badly burned also means less recognizeable. As long as I can't attach a face to the victim, I think I'd be alright.||||reg||7
Z||000015||loafer87gt||07-17-2001||01:00 PM||||Sounds like a hell of an idea. I know for a fact that there is a severe shortage of forensic anthropologists here in Canada. During my second year archeology we had a criminial forensic archeologist come in and speak with us and there were only two people in the country with his qualifications! Have you had a chance to take the archeology field methods class yet? Its a six week field exercise class that includes site survey, excavation, and lab analysis. It sound really interesting and would be a chance to get some hands on experience. I should have all the prerequisites this year and be able to take it next summer. Best of luck to you next fall.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||84Bill||07-17-2001||01:41 PM||||Degloving?<BR>So thats why fighter pilots wear a jumpsuit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000017||Mach10||07-17-2001||03:25 PM||||Boooooo!||||reg||13
Z||000018||Ken Wittlief||07-17-2001||03:33 PM||||I was jsut thinking of something else too. Ive done a lot of reading on ADD - some people look at it as having a brain that is wired differently - most people are wired in a way that makes then natural farmers - every year things are mostly the same - focus on one thing at a time - the seasons come in a fixed order - a place for everything and everything in its place<P>  the other 10% or so, people we label as having ADD or HADD are wired in a way that makes them natural hunters - aware of many things all at the same time, the temp of the air, the humidity, the wind direction and speed, the angle of the sun and the shadows, the sound of the wind in the trees, the slight noise of an animal in the distance, a faint scent in the air that you dont really smell - its more like you feel it.<P>  they take everything in all at once, if one thing changes they are immidiately aware of it<P>  every hunt is different, each time out is a new challenge, always on your toes, always ready for the unexpected.<P>  and in a strange twist of wording - instead of being attention defecient - when they are in hunt mode they are able to hyper-focus for hours at a time - days at time even - almost as if they are obsessed with a single task or goal or function - stay on the trail - stay on the hunt - dont let off for a second<P> people with ADD make excellent pilots - photographers - detectives and police officers - military personnel - long distance sailors - athletes<P>  but unfortunately our education system is set up for the farmers, not for the hunters - a sound outside a classroom window, someone walking by the door, any little thing distracts the hunter type from paying attention to ONLY what the teacher is doing right now. Have you ever looked at the hundreds of things they hang in 1st grade classrooms - like every square inch of wall is covered with something different - then they wonder why ADD kids dont pay attention?!<P>   so from that perspective I think this could be a great career for an ADD personality - always doing something new - each job is different - hundreds of different things to be aware of all at the same time. Every case is unique - and when you go to work in the morning you never know what to expect.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Ken Wittlief||07-17-2001||03:39 PM||||I was gonna say too - I remember people talking about the smell of death - and I always wondered what it was<P> from my experience the closest thing I can think of is somewhere between sour milk and swiss cheese - it makes me think of being on the waste side of a cheese factory. Thats not it exactly, but its the closest thing I can think of.<P> but there is also something about it that gets into your nose and throat, and even after you walk away you can still feel it in there - like it lingers with you longer than it should.||||reg||1
Z||000020||84Bill||07-17-2001||04:00 PM||||You forgot one Ken, Computer Technicians  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> though I was never diagnosed with ADHD but by definition I know I suffered from it while in school. Your right about the ability to focus everything into one fine point and not let go until problem solved. and BTW when I'm "in tune" I have predicted some unpleasant events I guess the kids at school were right, I am a weirdo  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000021||Mach10||07-17-2001||04:38 PM||||Ken: Thank you. I don't think ANYONE has ever bothered to try and explain what it's like in my head. Not my parents, my teachers, my shrink, or my practicioner. I live in a spiralling blur swirling down into a center of consciousness. Every bird, branch or flash of color is a stumbling block. Reading texts is an excersize of brute will power, rather than an attempt at self-learning. I can hyperfocus on things that interest me greatly (Fieros, computer games, Terry Pratchett novels) and stay on them indefinitely. But I can't choose what to hyperfocus on. It's like a tool that will fit any nut or bolt, but you can't pick which one to work on, and you can't choose whether to tighten or loosen.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Ken Wittlief||07-17-2001||05:11 PM||||exactly - if you can hyperfocus on something you are interested in the its a gift<P> they now think a lot of famous inventors and scientists, like edison and einstein, had ADD, or whatever you want to call it.<P>  I think its only considered a disorder now because of the way our education system is set up - which feeds on itself cause people with ADD end up HATING school, and once they get out they never want to have anything to do with it again - so they dont become part of the education system.<P>   someday all that will change and people that can hyperfocus and be hyperaware will be considered gifted instead of having a disorder.<P>  i dont know what side effects you suffered from ritalin, the most common one is feeling down and irritable when it wears off - but it does help the square pegs sit in the round hole for now.<P>   I always get a laugh out of people that think ritalin is somehow used to sedate kids with ADD - cause its a stimulant - its the same drug that was called 'speed' on the streets back in the '60s.<P>   they always miss that subtle fact when they slam docs and parents for giving ritialin to hyperactive kids to 'dope them up'.<P>   So let me get this straight - the kid is alreay hyperactive - wired! - and you give him a small amount of a STIMULANT - and it allows him to CALM DOWN and focus on one thing and sit still for more than 10 minutes at a time...<P>   give that same stimulant to a normal kid and he will be bouncing off the walls...<P>   but they insist there is nothing to all this ADD nonsense - the kid just needs to 'buckle down' and behave himself.<P>   Go figure! Someday we'll get it right and an educational system will be developed for the hunter types - and then you will see a serious outpouring of new creativity and human accomplishment.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Mach10||07-17-2001||06:09 PM||||Actually, I didn't have any "Coming Down" sideeffects. My problems were the firt 30 minutes, after taking the best working dosage: 30mg, 3 hours, 15mg. The first half hour, when the stuff hit my brain would cause a "high" that got quite annoying. Think along the lines of a "buzz" after slamming 3 shots of whiskey in a row. Wicked buzz, which tapers off rapidly. Kind of like that. Basically, I'd be too busy smiling at everything and feeling neat to get any work done. My doctor believes I need something stronger and slower acting, like dexederin, which my uncle is on. I said "cool" and never filled the prescription. I've learned a few tricks to help myself without the meds. <BR>It's actually good that I was diagnosed. The benefit comes in 2 parts:<BR>1) I know that it's not totally my fault. I'm not the only one who has these difficulites. This is a good morale boost for a student sinking into depression due to dismal first-year performance.<BR>2) Having a "target" (my ADD problem) also allows one to take responsibility for one's own actions. KNOWING what my problem is a large part of fixing it. Ok, not fixing. I like the way my mind works. I'm fast on the uptake, and REALLY good at complex problem solving (offset by clinicaly proven profound retardation in math  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> ). So, you just learn to cope. In my case, I'm a warehouse of small bits of information. Wow, coincidence, so's Anthropology... So it's not really a fluke that I'm persuing the career that I am. I found what I am really good at, and am shaping my future around it. Works for me.<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000024||Ken Wittlief||07-18-2001||10:28 AM||||unless you are a really big guy, 30mg seems on the high side.<P> if ritalin is 'working' for you, you dont feel like you are getting a buzz from it - one positive indication is if you feel like you are furrowing your eyebrows when you arnt - as if somehow your thinking has moved to the front of your forehead (i know how stupid that sounds but I dont know how else to describe it)<P>  also ritalin will help a quiet ADD person be more connected with other people - better able to carry on a converstaion without seeming like you have something else on your mind the whole time. ADD people often have weak social interaction skills.<P>  but the biggest benefit is when you have to sit and do something that normally does not grab your interest, maybe in your case, like math stuff - instead of feeling like you need to get up every 15 minutes and take a walk, our outright feeling like you want to get up and run away, you find yourself sitting down at a task you are not all that interested in and sticking with it for and hour or two at a time.<P>  I think you will find the most sucess if you treat stuff like math and other things that you cant get 'into' like an artist would. Artists are interested in creating, designing, expressing something about themselves. Often to do so they must be skilled musicians, carpenters, welders, painters, or even engineers, to be able to put their ideas into a physical form.<P>  So they learn the skills, the math, the chemistry, whatever it takes, whatever means they need to get to the end they envision - having to sit down and learn calc so you can implement a closed loop feedback control system, that will allow some electromechanical device to move in the manner you want, is like eating a rust sandwhich, and washing it down with a big frosty glass of sand - but they get through it.<P>  and surprizingly, sometimes they understand the math better that the mathemeticians do - but they hate it so they dont dwell on it. <P>  if ritalin is not working for you, you might at least try one other med and see if its better - but in the long run the best way to go is to only use the meds to get throught the things that soceity and our education system imposes on you in ways that go against your nature - and then find your own way of doing stuff that fits your nature, without the meds.<P> that will take some trial and error - sometimes you will feel like you are slacking off, or getting nowhere - or other people will tell you to stop screwing off all the time - but once you find your own path you will be able to do stuff that 'normal' people will never be able to accomplish.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Ken Wittlief||07-18-2001||10:35 AM||||just for example - I have an uncle who is a farmer type - but he went deer hunting for something like 12 years, and never brought home a single deer.<P>  and not cause there are no deer around here, casue a year after he gave it up, he hit one with his car<P> the other side of this whole thing is there is stuff that you can do, that you take for granted, that others cant - you probally dont even realize these things yet.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Mach10||07-19-2001||03:41 AM||||I ain't huge, no. 5'9, 180lbs.<P>3x 15mg tablets per day. 2 in the morning, 1 in early afternoon to maintain. That worked great for the longest time. One every 3 hours just doesn't seem to have the proper effect. I never had the furrowing thing. when it works, about 20 mins after dose, it's almost like a rushing sound, then everything comes into focus. That's how I describe it. Everything slows down for me, and I can organize my thoughts. This is, of course, if I don't get jittery. It varies, day-day. I seem to be able to cope with it now, so meds aren't really a priority to me. If things start going wrong, that's when I look for the pale blue pill.<P>Excuse my typing. I think I may have broken my hand falling off the porch. Will see if I can move it tommorrow  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>||||reg||7
